Requirements: 8   with obligations: 8

[task-01] The `acceptance` tool runs the reviewed project's tests with its own Python
    interpreter, from the directory it was launched in. A project whose tests live in a
    subdirectory with their own `pyproject.toml` and virtualenv therefore has none of
    its tests run: the tool's interpreter lacks the project's dependencies, and the test
    names pytest reports do not match the names the tool asks for, so every test is set
    aside as one pytest "declines".
    -> use-tool-interpreter  [functional/explicit]
       The acceptance tool runs the reviewed project's tests with its own Python
       interpreter.
    -> cwd-launch-directory  [functional/explicit]
       The acceptance tool runs from the directory it was launched in.
    -> subdir-tests-not-run  [functional/explicit]
       A project whose tests live in a subdirectory with their own `pyproject.toml` and
       virtualenv has none of its tests run.
    -> project-dependencies-available-to-tool-interpreter  [invariant/explicit]
       The tool's interpreter has the project's dependencies available.
    -> pytest-name-mismatch  [regression/explicit]
       The test names pytest reports do not match the names the tool asks for.
    -> pytest-declines-all-tests  [regression/explicit]
       Every test is set aside as one pytest "declines".

[task-02] Instead, the user tells the tool once, per repository, the command they
    already use to run their tests — for example `cd api && .venv/bin/python -m pytest`
    — and the tool runs the project's tests with exactly that command, adding only which
    tests to run. The tool does not guess an interpreter or a directory. When no test
    command is set and the tests cannot be run, the tool says that a test command needs
    to be set, rather than reporting that pytest declines the tests.
    -> test-command-provided-once-per-repo  [functional/explicit]
       The user tells the tool once, per repository, the command they already use to run
       their tests.
    -> run-tests-with-user-command-only-add-test-selection  [functional/explicit]
       The tool runs the project's tests with exactly the user-provided command, adding
       only which tests to run.
    -> no-guess-interpreter-or-directory  [invariant/explicit]
       The tool does not guess an interpreter or a directory.
    -> test-command-required-message  [functional/explicit]
       When no test command is set and the tests cannot be run, the tool says that a
       test command needs to be set.

[task-03] Tests run this way run as they do on the user's own machine: with the user's
    environment variables and with network access, so a suite that needs a local
    database and a variable such as `DATABASE_URL` can run.
    -> run-tests-with-env-and-network  [compatibility/explicit]
       Tests run as they do on the user's own machine, with the user's environment
       variables and with network access.
    -> local-db-and-env-var-test-runs  [functional/explicit]
       A suite that needs a local database and a variable such as `DATABASE_URL` can
       run.

[task-04] Because the tool runs the tests many times, including against deliberately
    broken copies of the code, setting the test command shows the user that risk in
    plain words and saves the command only after the user explicitly agrees. If the
    saved command changes, the user is asked to agree again. Each run prints the test
    command it uses and the names, never the values, of the environment variables passed
    to the tests. The tool never runs two copies of the project's tests at the same
    time.
    -> show-risk-in-plain-words  [explanation_observability/explicit]
       Setting the test command shows the user that risk in plain words.
    -> save-command-only-after-explicit-agreement  [functional/explicit]
       The test command is saved only after the user explicitly agrees.
    -> reconfirm-on-command-change  [functional/explicit]
       If the saved test command changes, the user is asked to agree again before the
       new command is saved.
    -> print-test-command-used  [explanation_observability/explicit]
       Each run prints the test command it uses.
    -> env-var-names-printed  [explanation_observability/explicit]
       Each test run prints the names of the environment variables passed to the tests.
    -> single-test-run-at-a-time  [invariant/explicit]
       The tool never runs two copies of the project's tests at the same time.

